Start by treating every time-related model as a projection of a deeper structure.

Base objects:

Proper time: (\tau)

Coordinate time: (t)

Metric / geometry: (g_{\mu\nu})

State: (x(t)) in some configuration space

Generic time functional: [ \mathcal{T} = \mathcal{T}\big(g_{\mu\nu}, x(t), \dot{x}(t), \text{fields}, \text{observers}\big) ] Then:

Classical time: (\mathcal{T} \to t) under flat metric, low velocity

Relativistic time: (\mathcal{T} \to \tau = \int \sqrt{-g_{\mu\nu} dx^\mu dx^\nu})

Cosmological time: (\mathcal{T} \to t_{\text{cosmic}}(a(t), \rho(t), \Lambda, ...))

So your “one equation of time” is not a single closed form, but a master functional whose special cases generate all familiar time equations.

